WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:07.380
Underwater splendor, thrilling action scenes,&nbsp;
and unbelievably human-looking animals. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:12.120
are the 12 moments in "Avatar: The Way of Water"&nbsp;
that showed us that James Cameron's still got it.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:17.640
Since there isn't any human business to get out&nbsp;
of the way in "Avatar 2," we start by catching up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:23.240
with the Sullys, who've been busy growing their&nbsp;
family. Jake, now fully and permanently Na'vi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:27.000
has gotten new dreadlocks and bulked up a&nbsp;
bit since he ran the military out of town.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.400
He has two natural-born sons, a baby&nbsp;
girl, and an adopted daughter, Kiri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.680
who was born of Grace's Avatar. For a moment, the&nbsp;
film stops on shots of their faces in close-up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.660
particularly one of Neytiri that's perfectly&nbsp;
symmetrical. These early glimpses of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.460
Sully family's life, and especially these&nbsp;
detailed shots of their faces show just how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.440
far the performance motion capture technology&nbsp;
Cameron uses has come in the last 13 years.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.540
As impressive as the Na'vi were in "Avatar,"&nbsp;
they still appeared to have been somewhat copied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:02.340
and pasted into backdrops at times, and their&nbsp;
movements weren't always entirely smooth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:07.140
believably real. The Na'vi in "Avatar: The Way&nbsp;
of Water" don't just look better integrated and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.300
more realistic. They look hyper-real, almost as&nbsp;
if there is a reality out there in space that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:18.040
crisper, clearer, more colorful, and more&nbsp;
high-resolution than our own could ever be.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:25.040
"Everything is backwards now. Like out there&nbsp;
is the true world and in here is the dream."

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:28.200
In the prologue, we learn that Jake, Neytiri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.860
and their brood have enjoyed years of&nbsp;
domestic contentment. During this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:37.020
they've been lulled into blissful ignorance as&nbsp;
to whether the sky people might ever return – but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.700
return they do. General Frances Ardmore fills&nbsp;
us in as to what's been happening on the human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.740
end for the last decade. It turns out Earth&nbsp;
is officially dying, and she's been sent to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:51.900
terraform Pandora to make it a suitable home&nbsp;
for the human race. Jake and Neytiri don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:56.340
know this yet. all they see are helicopters&nbsp;
and mechs burning down their beloved forests.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.780
A deeply distraught Neytiri cries out as&nbsp;
she did at the end of the first "Avatar"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.860
upon seeing history repeat itself.&nbsp;
Though what's happening is devastating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.700
the visual effects are astounding.&nbsp;
It's even more effective because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.479
we don't expect an action set piece&nbsp;
on this scale so early in the movie.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.460
When news broke that Stephen Lang had been&nbsp;
cast in "Avatar: The Way of Water," fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:22.860
justifiably wondered how that was possible. His&nbsp;
character, Colonel Miles Quaritch, died at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:28.620
point of Neytiri's arrow in that movie's climactic&nbsp;
battle. Lang is back, this time in the form of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:34.200
Recombinant — an Avatar spy without the need for a&nbsp;
human to control it. Quaritch's memories had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:39.660
uploaded as a type of consciousness into this blue&nbsp;
ten-foot-tall body. It sure seems like Quaritch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:44.700
being resurrected as the thing he hates is setting&nbsp;
him up for a Darth Vader arc in future "Avatar"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.560
installments, but for now, it's fun to watch&nbsp;
Quaritch prepare for his mission in the first act.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:54.120
As the team is about to arrive on Pandora,&nbsp;
Quaritch the Recombinant is let out of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:58.500
pod-like chamber. He floats around in&nbsp;
zero gravity for a while before finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.780
a high-tech tablet-like device. A video diary&nbsp;
recorded before human Quaritch's death explains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:08.340
to the reborn Quaritch who he is now, how&nbsp;
he came to be, and what he's meant to do.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:15.000
"I know you're all asking yourselves&nbsp;
the same question. Why so blue?"

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.460
Over a decade after mating for life, Jake and&nbsp;
Neytiri still clearly have the hots for each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:25.380
other. At one point, Jake narrates about how much&nbsp;
he looks forward to date night. For the Na'vi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:29.160
the height of romance is side-by-side&nbsp;
banshee flying just before the eclipse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.520
when Pandora's blacklight qualities&nbsp;
are at their most perceptible.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:36.000
Jake and Neytiri each take their&nbsp;
bonded banshee out for a spin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.960
The sequel gives us about a minute of&nbsp;
our favorite blue lovebirds carefree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:44.100
as they careen around the Hallelujah&nbsp;
Mountains and through Pandora's dense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:49.140
lit-up flora. It makes all the difference&nbsp;
that this isn't an escape or a mid-air melee.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:55.320
"Avatar: The Way of Water" has more of everything,&nbsp;
including subplots. The one that dominates the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.320 --> 00:04:00.660
middle third of the movie is troublemaking son&nbsp;
Lo'ak's relationship with the outcast bull Tulkun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:05.160
Payakan. A subplot within this subplot&nbsp;
is the mystery of how Payakan lost one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.540
of his fins. The first few times Lo'ak&nbsp;
and Payakan communicate, the whale-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:14.700
creature is reluctant to let the Na'vi boy&nbsp;
in on his secret. Eventually, Lo'ak earns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.620
Payakan's trust, and it becomes necessary for&nbsp;
the supposedly killer Tulkun to tell the truth.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:24.480
When the two bond, Lo'ak can see the&nbsp;
Tulkun's memories. The flashback is brief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.740
but the images of limp, dead Tulkuns&nbsp;
and Na'vi floating in flaming water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:33.120
littered with ships and shrapnel affect&nbsp;
the audience as much as the young Na'vi.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:40.020
Just like 2009's "Avatar," 2022's "Avatar: The&nbsp;
Way of Water" ends with a battle between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:44.880
Na'vi and the humans that's so monumental in&nbsp;
scale and length that it's hard to call it an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:49.860
ending. The nearly hour-long sequence aboard&nbsp;
and around a commandeered RDA whaling vessel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:54.540
is practically its own movie. Since it's&nbsp;
so involved, it's a fool's errand to pick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:59.040
out a single frame or an isolated incident&nbsp;
that shocks and awes more than the others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.900
One of the better action set pieces within the&nbsp;
epic last act might have reminded viewers of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:08.400
another iconic moment from James Cameron's&nbsp;
filmography — the sinking of the Titanic.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.380
Jake is about to surrender to Quaritch to save&nbsp;
his kids, but the Pandoran whale gives him the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:18.600
chance to attack instead. There are minutes upon&nbsp;
minutes of intensity that rival or surpass any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:23.940
action movie of the last 10 years in the scenes&nbsp;
that follow, but the tension really ramps up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:28.560
when everyone realizes the whaling vessel&nbsp;
is going to sink. Structures begin to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:34.500
apart. Water gushes into hallways. Characters&nbsp;
slide along surfaces and are sucked into vents.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.820
As in "Titanic," the sinking ship in&nbsp;
"Avatar: The Way of Water" manages to feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:43.832
claustrophobic and like the most bombastic&nbsp;
action sequence ever at the same time.

00:05:43.832 --> 00:05:47.760
Though "Avatar: The Way of Water" ends&nbsp;
with its longest and most complex battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:53.160
it kicks off with one of the franchise's most&nbsp;
visually and narratively interesting conflicts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:58.980
date. After a short prologue, a title card tells&nbsp;
us that a year has gone by since RDA spaceships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:04.440
reappeared in Pandora's skies. Jake, leader&nbsp;
of the Omaticaya, has been organizing guerilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:09.240
assaults every time General Ardmore's forces&nbsp;
breach their perimeter. This time, a high-speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:13.740
train with a cache of weapons and technology&nbsp;
is en route through Omaticaya territory.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:18.540
The coordinated Na'vi attack begins as they&nbsp;
bomb the track, derail the supply train,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:22.920
and loot the wreckage. This scene&nbsp;
wraps up as Jake's sons disobey him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:26.400
and abandon their lookout positions&nbsp;
to join the warriors on the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:32.400
but before the family drama, there's plenty&nbsp;
of mesmerizing 3D-enhanced action. It feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:36.080
like Jake's insurgency is going to&nbsp;
explode right out of the screen.

00:06:36.199 --> 00:06:38.539
"I am a warrior like you, i'm supposed to fight"

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:43.860
Lo'ak and Payakan are arguably the main characters&nbsp;
of "Avatar: The Way of Water," even though the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:47.700
overarching plot revolves around Jake trying&nbsp;
to protect his family while he's unknowingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:53.880
pushing away his son. After Lo'ak picks a fight&nbsp;
with Tonowari and Ronal's kids, Jake makes them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:59.040
apologize and tells them to play nice with the&nbsp;
people who've taken them in. Lo'ak tries, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.320
Metkayina boys are pretty bigoted toward forest&nbsp;
dwellers. They lure Jake and Neytiri's second-born&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:09.720
son to what they insist is a good fishing spot&nbsp;
and, as a sick joke, all but leave him for dead.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:14.340
The scene in which the boy tries to evade&nbsp;
Pandora's version of a shark is terrifying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:18.780
like something out of "Aliens" or "The&nbsp;
Abyss." A fade to black makes it seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:21.960
as though Lo'ak has drowned,&nbsp;
but he's rescued by Payakan.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.160
The scene that follows, in which Lo'ak&nbsp;
helps to remove a harpoon from Payakan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:29.400
fin and forms a relationship with&nbsp;
the blue whale-sized creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:33.900
is one of the most beautiful and emotional&nbsp;
in all of "Avatar: The Way of Water." The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:38.280
use of light coming through the water, the&nbsp;
realism of every splash, and the difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:43.420
in size between these two "brothers" make&nbsp;
these fleeting peaceful moments stand out.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:48.540
In a gripping but difficult-to-watch sequence,&nbsp;
Captain Mick pursues Ronal's soul sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:53.160
who'd recently given birth to a calf.&nbsp;
After deploying a fleet of boats and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:58.500
submarines armed with sound cannons, darts with&nbsp;
floatation devices, and exploding harpoons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:03.960
Ronal's Tulkun dies and her brain matter is&nbsp;
harvested as a human anti-aging substance.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:09.240
The next hunt doesn't go as well for Captain&nbsp;
Mick. When a tracking dart lands in Payakan and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:14.340
the Na'vi come to his rescue, the vengeful&nbsp;
Tulkun takes matters into his own fins. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:19.080
destroys the ship by wrapping the harpoon&nbsp;
cord around it and thrashing, resulting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:24.360
the aerial dismemberment of Captain Mick's arm.&nbsp;
These scenes aren't exactly beautiful, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:29.067
are no less visually masterful than anything&nbsp;
else "Avatar: The Way of Water" has to offer.

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:35.040
The exotic, colorful forests of Pandora made&nbsp;
a lasting impression on fans of "Avatar." The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:39.540
production design is probably the thing audiences&nbsp;
remember most (and most fondly) about the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:44.280
To keep things fresh, Cameron has added to&nbsp;
Pandora's worldbuilding with the Metkayina reef,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:49.560
part of a tropical coastal system of Na'vi clans&nbsp;
far from Jake and Neytiri's corner of the moon.

00:08:50.088 --> 00:08:52.980
As the Sully family approaches what&nbsp;
they hope will be their refuge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:58.920
they see misty mountains beset by tree roots&nbsp;
that extend into clear green-blue water. Na'vi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:03.840
children play in rocky tide pools that resemble&nbsp;
Pandora versions of something humans might see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:08.280
at Yellowstone. Three Brothers Rock protrudes&nbsp;
out of nowhere like monoliths from the Pacific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:13.080
Northwest. The Metkayina are loosely based&nbsp;
on Indigenous cultures such as the Māori,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:16.920
and production designers have brought&nbsp;
their world to life with texture and depth.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:20.340
When seen in 3D, viewers might be&nbsp;
tempted to get out of their seats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:24.360
and try to bounce around the Metkayina's&nbsp;
net bridges or take a bite out of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:28.680
crystalline fruit. Everything about the&nbsp;
reef makes one want to vacation there.

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:33.780
As "Avatar: The Way of Water" winds down,&nbsp;
it might feel like there's no possible way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:38.040
for Cameron to top himself. By this point,&nbsp;
we've seen a train heist, a shark attack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:43.080
and a guy's arm getting ripped off by a magical&nbsp;
whale. We've seen more banshees than we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:48.240
count. We've watched Jake and his family learn to&nbsp;
bond and ride with Ilu, and we're no longer blown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:54.180
away by scuffles between arrow-wielding Na'vi and&nbsp;
humans in mech suits. Then, for far too brief an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:58.920
instant, Jake Sully and Miles Quaritch engage&nbsp;
each other in a fight above the reef waters.

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:02.700
What's exciting about Jake and Quaritch's&nbsp;
unfinished duel is that it's an earned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:06.660
moment of rising action that's part of&nbsp;
what will be a trilogy-long conflict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:11.340
and it looks so much better than these&nbsp;
kinds of fantasy sky battles usually look.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:16.380
Let's be honest, the "Avatar" movies are just&nbsp;
as much about allowing audiences to visit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:21.300
Pandora as they are about telling a story.&nbsp;
So, when that story stops altogether after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:25.500
the Metkayina decides to grant the Sullys&nbsp;
refuge so that everybody can just hang out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:30.000
it's not only fine, it's the best part of&nbsp;
the movie. For about the next half hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.700
we learn to free dive with Tuk and Kiri. Sure, the&nbsp;
Sullys need to hone these skills if they're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:40.520
to live among the Metkayina, but this is really&nbsp;
an excuse for Cameron to show us what he can do.

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:43.280
"Just Breathe."

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:48.120
The underwater environments of "Avatar: The Way of&nbsp;
Water" and the scenes that take place in them look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:53.520
so phenomenal they'll likely change filmmakers'&nbsp;
concept of what's possible with 3D storytelling.

